        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains February 13 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, like 2 of 4.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For February 13 in music history, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>That is the Hot 100 chart week that includes February 13.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"One Bad Apple" had the longest run, with five weeks, and "Stayin' Alive" and "Da Ya Think I'm Sexy?" tie for second with four weeks each.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week, and four of those had only that one week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and {{N_LAST}} was in the final week of its run.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday, not only February 13 in music history.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the February 13 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called February 13.</p>
        <p>This February 13 in music history page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains February 13 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">February 13 in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Dutch Debut, a Five-Week Teen Hit and a Soul Classic</h2>
        <p>The first three Februaries of the decade brought a Dutch rock band, a family act and a soul singer.</p>

        <h3>1970: "Venus" by Shocking Blue</h3>
        <p>Robbie van Leeuwen, the band's guitarist, wrote "Venus."</p>
        <p>He wrote new lyrics to music based on Tim Rose and the Big 3's "The Banjo Song."</p>
        <p>It came out in the Netherlands on July 14, 1969, on the Pink Elephant label.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was the first song by a Dutch band to reach number one on the Hot 100.</p>
        <p>The written lyrics misspelled "goddess" as "godness," and Mariska Veres sang the misspelled word on the record.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of February 7, 1970.</p>
        <p>The song is number 33 on the 1970 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>Bananarama's 1986 version also reached number one in the US, according to Wikipedia.</p>

        <h3>1971: "One Bad Apple" by the Osmonds</h3>
        <p>George Jackson wrote "One Bad Apple" with the Jackson 5 in mind.</p>
        <p>Donny Osmond said Michael Jackson told him the group nearly recorded it first but chose "ABC" instead.</p>
        <p>The Osmonds recorded it on October 26, 1970, at FAME Studios in Muscle Shoals, Alabama, with Rick Hall producing.</p>
        <p>Hall had each brother sing the opening line, and Merrill and Donny were chosen.</p>
        <p>It came out on November 14, 1970, on MGM Records, from the album Osmonds.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one on February 13, 1971, and stayed there for five weeks.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with five weeks from February 13 to March 13.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 13 was the first of five.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1971 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1972: "Let's Stay Together" by Al Green</h3>
        <p>Al Green, Willie Mitchell and Al Jackson Jr. wrote "Let's Stay Together."</p>
        <p>Mitchell produced and recorded it, and the single came out in November 1971 on Hi Records.</p>
        <p>It topped Billboard's R&amp;B chart for nine weeks.</p>
        <p>Rolling Stone ranked it number 60 on its 2004 list of the 500 Greatest Songs of All Time.</p>
        <p>The Grammy Hall of Fame inducted it in 1999, and the Library of Congress added it to the National Recording Registry in 2010.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of February 12, 1972.</p>
        <p>The song is number 11 on the 1972 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/al-green-lets-stay-together/">Let's Stay Together</a> page, and more of his records are on <a href="/blog/artists/al-green/">Al Green</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">February 13 in Music History, 1973 to 1975: A Rock and Roll Joke, an Orchestral Instrumental and a Fire Siren</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade brought a retro rock song, an instrumental with no lead singer and a funk record that opens with a siren.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Crocodile Rock" by Elton John</h3>
        <p>Elton John and Bernie Taupin wrote "Crocodile Rock."</p>
        <p>The US release came on November 20, 1972, ahead of the album Don't Shoot Me I'm Only the Piano Player.</p>
        <p>A Farfisa organ riff, played by John, drives the record, and he sang all the vocals, including the falsetto backing parts.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it was inspired by Daddy Cool's "Eagle Rock," which John heard on his 1972 Australian tour.</p>
        <p>In 1974, Buddy Kaye, who composed "Speedy Gonzales," sued over the falsetto chords, and the case was settled and dismissed.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one on February 3, 1973, John's first US number one, and stayed there for three weeks.</p>
        <p>The chart week that contains February 13 was the second of the three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 7 on the 1973 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>More of his records are on <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a>.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Love's Theme" by the Love Unlimited Orchestra</h3>
        <p>Barry White wrote and produced "Love's Theme."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says he wrote it in 1965, but it was not recorded until 1973, at Whitney Recording Studios in Glendale, California.</p>
        <p>It came out in November 1973 on 20th Century Records.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it one of the few instrumental, purely orchestral singles to reach number one.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one on Billboard's Easy Listening chart.</p>
        <p>Black Box sampled it in "Ride on Time" in 1989.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of February 9, 1974.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1974 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Fire" by the Ohio Players</h3>
        <p>Seven members of the Ohio Players share the writing credit for "Fire," and the band produced it.</p>
        <p>It was recorded at Paragon Studios in Chicago and released in November 1974 on Mercury Records.</p>
        <p>A fire truck siren is heard at the start and again in the instrumental break.</p>
        <p>While the band was performing in California, Stevie Wonder heard the basic track and predicted it would be a big hit.</p>
        <p>It reached number one on both the Hot 100 and the Hot Soul Singles chart in early 1975.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows one week at number one, the chart week of February 8, 1975.</p>
        <p>The song is number 31 on the 1975 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>It was also the theme song for the first 18 seasons of the TV show Hell's Kitchen.</p>

        <h2 id="late">February 13 in Music History, 1976 to 1979: A Breakup Song, a Love Triangle and Two Late-Decade Giants</h2>
        <p>The last four Februaries brought a song about leaving, a song about staying and two of the biggest records of 1978 and 1979.</p>

        <h3>1976: "50 Ways to Leave Your Lover" by Paul Simon</h3>
        <p>Paul Simon wrote the song, and he and Phil Ramone produced it for the album Still Crazy After All These Years.</p>
        <p>It came out on December 20, 1975, on Columbia Records, the same day it entered the Hot 100 at number 74.</p>
        <p>Steve Gadd's drum pattern is one of its most recognizable features.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia notes that although the title says 50 ways, the lyrics mention only five.</p>
        <p>It peaked at number one on February 7, 1976, and stayed there for three weeks.</p>
        <p>It was Simon's only number one on that chart as a solo act.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with three weeks from February 7 to February 21.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 13 was the first of three.</p>
        <p>The song is number 8 on the 1976 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1977: "Torn Between Two Lovers" by Mary MacGregor</h3>
        <p>Peter Yarrow of Peter, Paul &amp; Mary and Phillip Jarrell wrote "Torn Between Two Lovers."</p>
        <p>Yarrow and Barry Beckett produced it, and MacGregor recorded it in 1976 at Muscle Shoals Sound Studio in Alabama.</p>
        <p>It came out in November 1976 on Ariola, from her debut album of the same name.</p>
        <p>It also reached number one on Billboard's Adult Contemporary chart and number four in the UK.</p>
        <p>A 1979 TV movie starring Lee Remick, George Peppard and Joseph Bologna took its title from the song.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data shows two weeks at number one, February 5 and February 12, 1977.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 13 was the second and last.</p>
        <p>The song is number 10 on the 1977 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Stayin' Alive" by the Bee Gees</h3>
        <p>Barry, Robin and Maurice Gibb wrote "Stayin' Alive" for the Saturday Night Fever soundtrack.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says they wrote it over a few days at the Ch&acirc;teau d'H&eacute;rouville, with more recording at Criteria Studios in Miami.</p>
        <p>It was released on December 15, 1977, on RSO Records.</p>
        <p>Drummer Dennis Bryon left the sessions early when his mother died, and a drum machine did not work.</p>
        <p>So the group took two bars from the "Night Fever" drum track and recorded them as a repeating loop.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it reached number one for the week of February 4, 1978, and stayed there four weeks.</p>
        <p>It was the second of the Bee Gees' six consecutive US number ones.</p>
        <p>The chart week that contains February 13 was the second of the four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/stayin-alive/">Stayin' Alive</a> page, and more of the group's records are on <a href="/blog/artists/bee-gees/">Bee Gees</a>.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Da Ya Think I'm Sexy?" by Rod Stewart</h3>
        <p>Rod Stewart, Carmine Appice and Duane Hitchings wrote the song, and Tom Dowd produced it.</p>
        <p>It was the first single from the album Blondes Have More Fun, released on November 10, 1978.</p>
        <p>The song borrows a melody from Jorge Ben Jor's "Taj Mahal," and Ben Jor sued over it.</p>
        <p>In his 2012 autobiography, Stewart called it "unconscious plagiarism."</p>
        <p>He also admitted to lifting the synth riff from an arrangement by Bobby Womack.</p>
        <p>The royalties went to UNICEF, and he performed it at the Music for UNICEF Concert in January 1979, the same concert as the <a href="/blog/trivia/january-9-in-music-history/">January 9 in music history</a> list covers.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says it spent four weeks at number one in February 1979.</p>
        <p>This site's weekly data agrees, with four weeks from February 10 to March 3.</p>
        <p>So the chart week that contains February 13 was the first of four.</p>
        <p>The song is number 4 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

